### Has the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) addressed the idea of using nuclear weapons to affect hurricanes, and if so, what is their scientific stance?
Answer: Yes, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) has addressed this idea, advising that "using nuclear weapons to destroy hurricanes is not a good idea" and warning that the results would be "devastating." Scientifically, the NOAA explains that a nuclear bomb "might not even alter the storm" because a hurricane's heat release is equivalent to a "10-megaton nuclear bomb exploding every 20 minutes." Furthermore, they warn that the "radioactive fallout would fairly quickly move with the tradewinds to affect land areas," and that attempting to target weaker tropical depressions beforehand is not promising since there is no way to predict in advance which ones will actually develop into hurricanes.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-49471093

